licence. Each changed file carries a "Modified by the QUICraft project" note.)

QUICraft patch for Netty netty-4.2.19.Final (commit 64cc10f38ea5f5bd7eae48507817c66680d0afdc),
on top of 0001-0004: caps on quiche's flow-control auto-tuning, which docs/protocol.md
section 13.13 needs before /3 may be enabled.

quiche grows each stream's and the connection's receive window automatically, by default up to
16 MiB per stream and 24 MiB per connection. Section 13.13 pins those maxima to 4 MiB and
17 MiB so that transport auto-tuning can never advertise credit beyond the receive budgets of
section 13.12. Netty sets the initial limits but has no setter for the maxima. This patch adds:

- JNI: Quiche.quiche_config_set_max_connection_window(config, bytes) and
  Quiche.quiche_config_set_max_stream_window(config, bytes), which call the quiche C functions
  of the same name.
- QuicCodecBuilder.maxConnectionWindow(long) and maxStreamWindow(long): positive values, copied
  by the builder's copy constructor and passed through QuicheConfig. Left unset, quiche keeps its
  own defaults, so existing callers behave exactly as before.
- Quic.isMaxFlowControlWindowsSupported(): probes the two bindings once, like
  isRelaxedLossThresholdSupported() from 0001. Both builder setters throw
  UnsupportedOperationException when it is false, so a caller on an upstream native library
  fails at configuration time instead of with an UnsatisfiedLinkError when a connection starts.

Nothing else changes, and the native jar gains no dependency.

Applied by natives/build-netty-quic.sh with `git apply`, which ignores this text.

